"
\h0#preface|Preface|

This document describes \c{ignorerevs}, a tool for maintaining the
\c{.git-blame-ignore-revs} files of git repositories. For the command line
interface refer to the \l{ignorerevs(1)} man page.

\h1#intro|Introduction|

The \c{ignorerevs} tool maintains the \c{.git-blame-ignore-revs} file of a
git repository. This file lists the commits that \c{git-blame(1)} should
skip when attributing lines to commits, normally commits that only change
formatting (such as a commit that fixes the indentation of a file).
GitHub uses this file by default and git uses it if the
\c{blame.ignoreRevsFile} configuration variable is set to it.

Such commits are easy to overlook if the file is maintained by hand,
especially the small ones (a stray tab fixed in passing, a file converted to
different line endings) that accumulate in a repository with a long
history. For example, the history of the IW4x client contains 35 commits
(out of more than five thousand) that only change whitespace.

The \c{ignorerevs} tool finds these commits in the history and lists them
in the file. It also keeps the commits that the file already lists, which
means that a reformat it does not recognize as such (for example, one that
also moves line breaks) can be added by hand.

We begin with a tutorial introduction that generates the
\c{.git-blame-ignore-revs} file of a small repository and then discuss what
is considered a whitespace change as well as the commits added by hand in
more detail.

\h1#start|Getting Started|

Let's start with a repository whose history has two commits that only
change formatting (an indentation fix and a reformat into a single line)
and one that changes what the program does:

\
$ cd hello/
$ git log --format='%h %s'
7211661 Return failure
e28ddab Join main into one line
f6cff4f Indent main
ba19f54 Add hello
\

The repository doesn't have \c{.git-blame-ignore-revs} yet, so let's run
\c{ignorerevs} and see what it would write:

\
$ ignorerevs

repository:         /src/hello
history commits:    4
whitespace commits: 1

+# Commits that git-blame(1) skips since they only change ...
...
+# Indent main
+f6cff4f0b9e84e07b0ea7d03a91b5147cca59b0f

update '/src/hello/.git-blame-ignore-revs'? [y/n]: y
\

The first lines show the number of commits in the history and how many of
them only change whitespace, one in our case. Next comes the new file in
the \c{diff(1)} notation. It starts with a comment that describes the file
(shortened here) followed by the commit, written as its full object name
with its subject in a comment above it (this is the customary format of
\c{.git-blame-ignore-revs}).

Once we answer \c{y}, \c{ignorerevs} installs the file and commits it with
the added commits listed in the commit message:

\
$ git log -1 --format=%B
Update .git-blame-ignore-revs

  add    f6cff4f Indent main
\

Notice that \c{e28ddab}, the reformat into a single line, is not in the
file. This commit moves line breaks and \c{ignorerevs} does not consider
that a whitespace change (see \l{#whitespace Whitespace Changes} for
details). To have \c{git-blame(1)} skip it as well, we add it to the file
by hand, using its abbreviated name, and run \c{ignorerevs} again:

\
$ echo e28ddab >>.git-blame-ignore-revs
$ git commit -am 'Ignore the one-line main'
$ ignorerevs

repository:         /src/hello
history commits:    6
whitespace commits: 1
commits by hand:    1

--- a/.git-blame-ignore-revs
+++ b/.git-blame-ignore-revs
@@ -6,4 +6,5 @@

 # Indent main
 f6cff4f0b9e84e07b0ea7d03a91b5147cca59b0f
-e28ddab
+# Join main into one line
+e28ddab1104f80742b4b9540f7dc979b23315b60

update '/src/hello/.git-blame-ignore-revs'? [y/n]: y
\

The added commit is written back with its full object name and subject and
from now on it is kept on every update. If we now set
\c{blame.ignoreRevsFile}, then \c{git blame} skips both formatting commits
and attributes the line to the commit that changed what the program
returns:

\
$ git config blame.ignoreRevsFile .git-blame-ignore-revs
$ git blame -s hello.cxx
72116613 1) int main () { return 1; }
\

The options of \c{ignorerevs} are the same as those of \c{mailmap} (see
\l{ignorerevs(1)} for details).

\h1#whitespace|Whitespace Changes|

To decide whether a commit only changes whitespace, \c{ignorerevs} compares
each file that the commit modifies as it was before the commit with how it
is after. Before the comparison, each line of both versions is normalized
by removing its leading and trailing whitespace and replacing each run of
whitespace inside it with a single space. Blank lines as well as carriage
returns at the end of lines are dropped. If the two versions of every file
are the same after normalization, then the commit only changes whitespace.

As a result, a commit that reindents code (including code that was not
indented before) or converts its line endings is a whitespace change. A
commit that adds or removes a space between two words (turning \c{int x}
into \c{intx}, say) is not.

Note that the \c{-b} and \c{-w} options of \c{git-diff(1)} are not quite
what we need here. With \c{-b} indenting a line that was not indented is a
change, since this option only ignores differences in the amount of
whitespace where there is some. And with \c{-w} the lines \c{int x} and
\c{intx} are the same, since this option ignores all whitespace. You can
see the former with the indentation fix from our walkthrough:

\
$ git diff -b f6cff4f^ f6cff4f
@@ -1,4 +1,4 @@
 int main ()
 {
-return 0;
+  return 0;
 }
\

Only commits that modify existing files are considered. If a commit adds or
removes files (a rename counts as both) or only changes the mode of a file,
then it is not a whitespace change. Merges are not considered at all (the
commits they merge are).

Commits that change files in which whitespace is significant are not
considered either. For example, indentation determines the structure of a
Python program and two spaces at the end of a line are a line break in
Markdown, so reformatting such a file may well change its meaning. See
\l{ignorerevs(1)} for the list of such file types.

\h1#hand|Commits Added by Hand|

A reformat that does more than change whitespace (such as one that wraps
long lines or sorts \c{#include} directives) is not recognized by
\c{ignorerevs}, since telling whether it changes the meaning of the code is
beyond a comparison of text. If you want \c{git-blame(1)} to skip such a
commit, then add it to the file by hand. Any name that git understands can
be used (for example, an abbreviated object name) since \c{ignorerevs}
writes the commit back with its full object name and subject. From then on
the commit is kept on every update.

A listed commit must be in the history of \c{HEAD}. If the history is
rewritten (for example, by a rebase that gives the listed commits new
object names), then \c{ignorerevs} lists the entries it can no longer find
and fails without changing anything. Such an entry has to be replaced with
the rewritten commit (or removed) by hand, since only a person can tell
whether the rewritten commit still only changes formatting.

A shallow clone is refused since its history is incomplete and every
listed commit older than the clone would appear to be missing.

\h1#architecture|How ignorerevs Works|

This chapter describes the file that \c{ignorerevs} writes.

\h#architecture-file|The File|

The file starts with a comment that describes it, followed by an empty line
and then the commits, from oldest to newest. Each commit is written as its
full object name (\c{git blame} refuses to run if the file contains an
abbreviated name that it cannot resolve) preceded by a comment with its
subject. Since both the order and the comments are derived from the
history, the same history and entries always produce the same file and an
up to date file is left alone.

If a repository has no commits that only change whitespace and no
\c{.git-blame-ignore-revs}, then it is left alone as well (there is no
reason to add a file that lists nothing).

Note that installing the file replaces it, which would turn a symbolic link
into a copy of its target. As a result, a \c{.git-blame-ignore-revs} that is
a symbolic link is refused and it is the file it refers to that should be
updated.
"
